```--------------------Since there is no "@@@" comment present in the provided code, I will write the Debug.Assert code based on the conditions described in the code.

Please note that for brevity, I will assume that there are three test cases: One when the item is added, when it is removed and when it is present multiple times. The code would look something like:

```cs
public void PUT_ContainsContract_WhenItemAdded([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
{
    arrList.Add(x);
    Debug.Assert(arrList.Contains(x));

    arrList.Remove(x);
    Debug.Assert(!arrList.Contains(x));

    arrList.AddRange(new int[] {x, x, x});
    Debug.Assert(arrList.Contains(x));
}
```

This code asserts that after adding an item, it will be contained, then after removing it, it will no longer be contained and after adding it again, will be contained.

Remember that these are basic examples and depending on the actual test, the Assert statements might need to change significantly. Debug.Assert works on certain condition, here it seems to be checking if added item is in the array while removing we are checking if removed item is not present. You need to change the condition as per your test logic.--------------------Here is the resulting C# code using the Debug.Assert method to verify the given predicate:

```cs
Debug.Assert(Old_arrListCount >= -1);
